/.net-in-my-browser /net-in-my-browser/
/.net-in-my-browser/ /net-in-my-browser/

# gatsby-plugin-sitemap 6 renamed its output: one sitemap.xml became
# sitemap-index.xml plus sitemap-0.xml. Keep the long-published URL alive.
# The target resolves through the root rewrite at the bottom of this file.
/sitemap.xml /sitemap-index.xml 301!
/tech-blog/sitemap.xml /sitemap-index.xml 301!

/tech-blog/admin/* /admin/:splat 301!
/tech-blog/admin/ /admin/ 301!
/tech-blog/admin /admin/ 301!
/tech-blog/a /admin/ 301!

https://tech.boldare.com/tech-blog/* https://boldare.com/tech-blog/:splat 301
https://tech.boldare.com/ https://boldare.com/tech-blog/ 301

# Under Gatsby 2 the build output was written unprefixed and this file mapped
# /tech-blog/* onto it. Gatsby 5 inverts that: the adapter prefixes every route
# with pathPrefix (gatsby/dist/utils/adapter/manager.js), so the deployed site
# genuinely lives at /tech-blog/ on this origin.
#
# The public site still needs the origin to answer at its root, because
# www.boldare.com proxies with the prefix STRIPPED:
#     /tech-blog/*  ->  https://tech.boldare.com/:splat  200
# (see nyc-spa static/_redirects). So a reader on www.boldare.com/tech-blog/foo/
# reaches this origin as /foo/, which no longer has a file behind it.
#
# This rewrite puts it back. Netlify only applies a non-forced rule when no file
# matches the request, so /tech-blog/* is still served directly from disk and
# only unprefixed paths fall through to here.
/* /tech-blog/:splat 200
